elds are exposed via Chi/Peng/Gang by others. You\u2019ve used \"Chi B5\" earlier, indicating the [B4,B5,B6] meld was formed by claiming B5 \u2014 so it's **exposed**.\n\nYour current hand: [W6, W9, T1, T2, T3, T3, F4, J1] plus two exposed melds.  \nYou need to minimize risk while avoiding feeding opponents\u2019 likely hands.\n\nObserve:\n- Next player has konged F2 and punged T8/B8 \u2014 possibly going for honors or upper numbers.\n- Across player has completed W1-W5 runs \u2014 possibly building Full Flush in Characters (W) or Pure Straight.\n- Previous player has full sets of J3, F2, W1 \u2014 very advanced hand, possibly close to winning.\n\nDangerous tiles: Avoid discarding **W9**, **T1**, **T2**, **T3** \u2014 these may feed chows in lower bamboo/dots/characters. Especially **T3**: opponent across discarded T1, T2, T5 \u2014 might be building something involving T3-T4-T5? Unlikely since T4/T5 are low, but still a risk.\n\nSafer discards: **F4** or **J1** \u2014 both honors, less likely to complete chows. Among them, **F4** is better to discard because:\n- Prevailing and Seat Wind is **North (F3)**, not West (F4). So F4 has no scoring value for anyone unless they specifically want a F4 pung (unlikely, as F4 hasn't been seen much).\n- Two F4 tiles remain unseen, but no one has shown interest.\n- J1 (Red Dragon): more universally useful; dragons score points easily (Dragon Pung = 2 pts, Two Dragon Pungs = 6 pts, etc.). One J1 discard already happened, but two remain \u2014 someone might be collecting.\n\nThus, **F4** is safer than J1.\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding **F4** minimizes the risk of feeding opponents' chows or pungs. It is not a prevalent or seat wind tile, and no player has shown interest in F4. Compared to suited tiles like T3 or W9, which could complete chows, F4 is the least dangerous honor tile to discard at this stage.\n\nAnswer: Play F4"}
